\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Constant-pressure trajectory through the quark conversion layer in the $(\mu _B,\mu _K)$ plane at fixed $T=10\,\text  {MeV}$. The red curve is the quark-matter isobar for $n_B(0^-)=3n_0$ and $B^{1/4}=189.1566\,\text  {MeV}$. The red arrows run from the just-deconfined state at $x=0^+$ to the equilibrated state at $x\to \infty $; the green arrow represents deconfinement from the nuclear-side marker at $x=0^-$. \textsf  {\color  {blue}[\textsuperscript  {LB}Can we make the x=0- to 0+ curve a straight line. It seems too suggestive that this is a particular path. Also are the blue arrows necessary?]}}}{6}{figure.caption.14}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:isobar_muB_muK}{{3}{6}{Constant-pressure trajectory through the quark conversion layer in the $(\mu _B,\mu _K)$ plane at fixed $T=10\,\MeV $. The red curve is the quark-matter isobar for $n_B(0^-)=3n_0$ and $B^{1/4}=189.1566\,\MeV $. The red arrows run from the just-deconfined state at $x=0^+$ to the equilibrated state at $x\to \infty $; the green arrow represents deconfinement from the nuclear-side marker at $x=0^-$. \LBcom {Can we make the x=0- to 0+ curve a straight line. It seems too suggestive that this is a particular path. Also are the blue arrows necessary?}}{figure.caption.14}{}}
